---
import BaseLayout from "../components/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import StatusBadge from "../components/StatusBadge.astro";
import Disclaimer from "../components/Disclaimer.astro";
import { diseases, photoTips } from "../data/learn";
import { href } from "../site.config";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Learn"
  description="What the detector labels mean, in plain language, with general pointers from FAO and university extension sources. Pending expert review."
>
  <PageHeader
    title="Learn"
    intro="Plain-language notes on what the detector labels mean, with general pointers from FAO and university extension sources. This page is pending expert review: an apiculture expert must check it before it is treated as guidance."
  >
    <p><StatusBadge kind="review" /></p>
  </PageHeader>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="notice notice-warn">
      <p>
        <strong>Pending expert review.</strong> An apiculture expert must review this page before
        it is treated as guidance. The pointers below are general information from the sources
        named, not prescriptions for any colony. If anything here conflicts with advice from a
        qualified apiary inspector or lab, follow the inspector or lab. Some bee diseases must be
        reported to authorities; check local rules.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>The four Stage 2 labels</h2>
    {diseases.map((disease) => (
      <article class="card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">{disease.label}</h3>
        <p><StatusBadge kind="review" /></p>
        {disease.image && (
          <figure>
            <img src={href(`/${disease.image.file}`)} alt={disease.image.alt} loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>
              {disease.image.caption} Credits: <a href={href("/credits")}>Image credits</a>.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        )}
        <h4>What the project report says</h4>
        <ul>
          {disease.reportNotes.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="small muted">Source: {disease.source}</p>
        {disease.guidance && (
          <>
            <h4>What to look for</h4>
            <ul>
              {disease.guidance.signs.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
            </ul>
            <h4>How it spreads</h4>
            <ul>
              {disease.guidance.spread.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
            </ul>
            <h4>First steps</h4>
            <ul>
              {disease.guidance.firstSteps.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
            </ul>
            <h4>Cautions</h4>
            <ul>
              {disease.guidance.cautions.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
            </ul>
            <p class="small muted">Source: {disease.guidance.source}</p>
          </>
        )}
      </article>
    ))}

    <h2>When to call an expert</h2>
    <p>
      This page helps you recognise possible signs. It cannot confirm a diagnosis. If you see any
      disease symptom, check it promptly with a veterinarian or bee-health specialist, and do not
      move frames, bees, or equipment to healthy colonies until the health status is known.
      Laboratory testing may be needed to confirm which disease is present.
    </p>
    <p>
      Seek specialist help urgently if many colonies are affected, if bees are dying in large
      numbers, if you suspect foulbrood, or if signs keep getting worse despite mite control and
      good husbandry. Spotty brood and Parasitic Mite Syndrome can resemble foulbrood: do not
      assume chalkbrood or Varroa from a photo alone — get specialist or laboratory confirmation.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Source: FAO (2020), Good beekeeping practices, Annex 1; Penn State Extension, Viruses in
      Honey Bees. Accessed 12 Nov 2026. Pending expert review.
    </p>

    <h2>Notifiable diseases</h2>
    <p>
      Varroosis (infestation with <em>Varroa</em> spp.) is listed by the World Organisation for
      Animal Health (WOAH) in the Terrestrial Animal Health Code. Deformed wing virus and
      chalkbrood are not on that WOAH list. WOAH notification duties apply to countries through
      their veterinary authorities. What a beekeeper must report, and to whom, depends on the
      veterinary regulations where the bees are kept — check the rules that apply to you and follow
      them. If you are unsure, ask a veterinarian or bee-health specialist promptly.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Source: WOAH Terrestrial Animal Health Code, Chapter 1.3 (2024 update), Article 1.3.2;
      Chapter 1.1 on notification. Accessed 12 Nov 2026. The expert review should confirm the
      current Code wording.
    </p>

    <h2>What a photo cannot tell you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Whether a colony is truly disease-free. A photo shows only what is visible in that frame.</li>
      <li>Early disease with no visible sign, internal disease, or queen problems.</li>
      <li>Anything about Nosema, foulbrood, or other conditions outside the four labels.</li>
      <li>How heavy a Varroa infestation is, or which virus is present. Those need mite counts or laboratory tests.</li>
      <li>What treatment, if any, is right. That needs an inspector or lab, and local rules.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Taking a usable photo</h2>
    <ul>
      {photoTips.map((tip) => <li>{tip}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">Photo guidance follows the report's input requirements (Table 4.1).</p>

    <Disclaimer />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
